# Animation rules

- Read docs/ANIMATION_DIRECTION.md before adding or changing animations.
- Use the shared presets in `client/src/lib/animationPresets.ts` (SLAM, POP, SETTLE,
  SCREEN, STAGGER) — do not invent new spring constants inline without reason.
- Animations must clarify game events, not hide them. Every effect answers
  "what just happened?". Decorative-only animation belongs in ambient layers.
- Reuse the game-feel toolkit before building new effects: EmojiBurst, ImpactFlash,
  FloatingText, Shaker, CannonBlast, LockStamp, SceneBackdrop, PirateAvatar,
  ItemCard, ItemUseOverlay (see docs/GAME_FEEL.md for the full table).
- All particle counts go through `particleCount(base, intensity)` and all screen shake
  through `shakeAllowed(intensity)` from `client/src/lib/gameFeel.ts`. Never bypass the
  intensity system.
- Ambient scene layers are `pointer-events-none`, sit behind content (z-index ≤ 1),
  and must gate on `ambientAllowed(intensity)`.
- Prefer Framer Motion for stateful transitions, CSS keyframes for infinite idle loops.
- Do not add heavy animation libraries (PixiJS, Lottie, GSAP) without explicit approval;
  if ever added, isolate behind a component boundary.
- Big sequences (chest, reveals) need a skip affordance after ~1s but should be built
  to be watched.
- Sounds: fire named events via `playSound()` from `client/src/lib/soundEvents.ts`,
  not raw `sfx.*` calls, in new code.
- Keep `prefers-reduced-motion` working: the global CSS collapse must not be removed,
  and critical information must never be motion-only.
